  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1020 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThe long term forecast will start off with a nice and pleasant \nThursday. Upper ridging will prevail overhead much of the day \nThursday, however the upper low off the coast of California will \nmove onshore effectively pushing the upper ridging to the east. This \nupper pattern change will bring precipitation chances and cooler \ntemperatures starting Friday and continue over the weekend. As the \nupper low shifts onshore, flow aloft will shift to the southwest \npumping in plenty of subtropical moisture. Precipitation chances \nwill start off across western portions of the Caprock Friday before \nexpanding east across the rest of the region Saturday and Sunday. \nThe greatest threat expected with these storms is heavy rain that \ncould lead to flooding with PWATS up to 1.25 inches. Daily showers \nand thunderstorms will cool temperatures off with highs reaching \nlower 60s by Sunday. Models indicate a cold front will push through \nregion Monday prolonging precipitation chances and cooler \ntemperatures. Current model runs differ with the frontal timing, \nalthough not by much. GFS has a faster track with the front pushing \nthrough Monday morning. ECMWF has a slower track with the front \npushing through Monday afternoon.",
